- Retrieved practices:
- Avoid information leakage when passing C++ class objects across trust boundaries
WHAT: Passing raw C++ objects (with padding, virtual bases, and bit-fields) across a trust boundary can leak uninitialized or sensitive data via padding bytes or unused bits.
WHY: Attackers in the untrusted context may read leaked data (keys, pointers, previous stack/heap contents), leading to information disclosure and aiding further exploitation.
HOW: Never memcpy or transmit whole objects directly to untrusted contexts; instead, explicitly serialize only the intended fields in a defined order and size, and ensure all serialized fields are fully initialized.
EXAMPLE (C++):
// Before (unsafe): std::memcpy(buf, &obj, sizeof(obj));  // leaks padding
// After (safe):   write_field(buf, obj.field1); write_field(buf, obj.field2);  // only explicit, initialized fields

- Mismatched language linkage in function calls
WHAT: Calling a function (or passing a function pointer) with a different language linkage than the callee expects (e.g., passing a C++ function pointer where "extern \"java\"" or "extern \"C\"" is required) causes undefined behavior at runtime.
WHY: Undefined behavior can lead to crashes, data corruption, or exploitable memory-safety vulnerabilities, especially when foreign runtimes or ABIs (e.g., Java interpreters, C libraries) are involved.
HOW: Ensure that function pointer types and declarations use the same language linkage as required by the callee; declare callbacks with the correct extern specification and avoid implicit conversions between differently linked function pointer types.
EXAMPLE: Before (noncompliant, C++): `extern "java" typedef void (*java_callback)(int); extern void call_java_fn_ptr(java_callback cb); void callback_func(int); void f(){ call_java_fn_ptr(callback_func); }` After (compliant): `extern "java" void callback_func(int); void f(){ call_java_fn_ptr(callback_func); }`

- Avoid invalid pointer-to-member access in C++
WHAT: Using .* or ->* with a pointer-to-member that refers to a member not present in the dynamic type of the object, or using a null pointer-to-member, causes undefined behavior.
WHY: Undefined behavior can lead to memory corruption, crashes, or exploitable security vulnerabilities.
HOW: Ensure the dynamic type of the object actually defines the targeted member before using .* or ->*; never use null pointer-to-member values; avoid casting pointer-to-member values across unrelated types.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `void (Base::*pm)() = nullptr; (obj.*pm)();` After (safe): `void (Base::*pm)() = &Base::f; if (pm) (obj.*pm)();`

- Do not use pointer-to-member operators with null or uninitialized member pointers
WHAT: Using the pointer-to-member operator (->* or .* ) with a null or uninitialized pointer-to-member invokes undefined behavior and may call an invalid function or access a nonexistent member.
WHY: Undefined behavior can lead to crashes, arbitrary code execution, or other unpredictable security vulnerabilities.
HOW: Always initialize pointer-to-member variables to valid members before use, and check that the pointer-to-member is non-null and points to an existing member before invoking it.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `static void (D::*gptr)(); void call_memptr(D *ptr){ (ptr->*gptr)(); }` After (safe): `static void (D::*gptr)() = &D::g; void call_memptr(D *ptr){ if (gptr) (ptr->*gptr)(); }`

- Do not leak resources when handling exceptions
WHAT: Failing to release dynamically allocated resources when an exception is thrown (for example, returning or rethrowing before delete/free) causes resource leaks.
WHY: Resource leaks can exhaust memory or other resources, leading to denial of service, instability, or degraded performance.
HOW: Use RAII (resource acquisition is initialization) objects (e.g., smart pointers, wrapper classes) so that destructors automatically release resources in all control paths, including exceptions; avoid raw new/delete in code that can throw.
EXAMPLE: Before (leaks on exception): `T* p = new T(); risky(); delete p;` After (no leak): `std::unique_ptr<T> p = std::make_unique<T>(); risky();`



